{
  "@context": "../../context.jsonld",
  "@type": "Process",
  "critical": false,
  "description": "Heat rejection system design for nuclear electric propulsion: a fission reactor producing 100-1000 kWe of electricity rejects 2-5 MW of waste heat, which can only be removed in space by radiation. Pumped-loop radiators (NaK or water coolant circulated through aluminum or titanium-polymer finned panels) require 50-500 m^2 of radiator area for typical system sizes. Advanced concepts include liquid-droplet radiators (a sheet of molten metal sprayed through space and recaptured, achieving 4-10x the heat-flux per unit mass) and heat-pipe radiators (no moving parts). Radiator area is the dominant mass and deployment constraint of any NEP architecture: at 100 kWe a pumped-loop radiator masses 5-10 tonnes, at 1 MWe the radiator dominates vehicle mass.",
  "early_win": false,
  "id": "space-propulsion.nuclear-electric.heat-radiator-design",
  "level": "process",
  "name": "Heat Radiator Design",
